# Debugging and tracing

```elixir
System.put_env("AL_MNESIA_DISTRIBUTED", "false")

System.put_env(
  "AL_MNESIA_DIR",
  Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "al_livebook_#{:erlang.unique_integer([:positive])}")
)

Mix.install(
  [{:al, github: "anoma/AL-Ex"}],
  config: [al: [transaction_programs: [AL.TransactionProgram.Bootstrap], natives: []]]
)
```

## Setup

This notebook is self-contained. The cell above installs AL directly, no separate node, no cookie, nothing to attach to. The first run compiles AL and its dependencies from scratch, which takes a minute or two. Reopening this notebook later is fast, since `Mix.install` caches by the exact dependency and config combination. State here is throwaway. It lives only for this notebook's session and disappears when the session ends.

If you already have a real AL node running and want the persistent, shared store instead, see the [intro](./intro.livemd) for how to attach to it instead.

`Mix.install` has to finish running before AL's macros (`run`, `defclass`, and the rest) can be brought into scope, so that happens in its own cell here.

```elixir
use AL

AL.Branch.head()
```

## Reading a failure

An aborted `run` comes back with a structured reason naming exactly what went wrong. Sending a selector a method it does not understand names the receiver, the selector, its arity, and a ranked list of suggestions.

```elixir
result =
  run do
    defclass :failgreeter, super: :value do
      defmethod(:init, [self, _, self])
      defmethod(:greet, [self, _name])
    end

    new(:failgreeter, g)
    greett(g, :world)
  end

{:aborted, reason} = result
{reason.reason, reason.message}
```

AL's reporter can also report domain violations.

```elixir
run do
  defclass :failure_domain_probe, super: :object, ivars: [state: [domain: ["on", "off"]]] do
  end

  new(:failure_domain_probe, %{name: :failure_domain_instance, state: "on"}, _)
end
```

## A custom `does_not_understand` suppresses the failure entirely

If a class handles its own misses, sending it something unknown doesn't abort. This allows for custom fallback behaviour.

```elixir
run do
  defclass :failquiet, super: :value do
    defmethod(:init, [self, _, self])
    defmethod(:does_not_understand, [self, _m, _a])
  end

  new(:failquiet, q)
  anything(q, :x)
end
```

## The domino tracing model

AL's trace is called "domino" internally, and and is built on the classic Prolog tracer model: a "Byrd box" for every call, with four ports — `Call`, `Exit`, `Redo`, `Fail`. Plain Prolog needs one box per predicate call. AL needs two, stacked: a **method** box (dispatch choosing which provider answers a selector), which wraps a **clause** box (which clause of the chosen method actually runs). Every send opens because AL has dispatch sitting on top of clause selection, whereas Prolog only has the latter.

The trace is always being recorded, cheaply, whether or not you ask to see it printed- `state.domino.trace` holds it on every run, traced or not.

```elixir
{:atomic, {bindings, state}} =
  run do
    fibonacci(3, x)
  end

state.domino.trace
|> Enum.reverse()
|> Enum.map(fn
  entry when is_tuple(entry) -> elem(entry, 0)
  entry -> entry
end)
|> Enum.filter(&(&1 in [:method_call, :method_exit, :clause_call, :clause_exit]))
```

Each of the three recursive `fibonacci` calls opens its own method box immediately wrapping a clause box, and closes them in the same nested order. `method_call, clause_call, ..., clause_exit, method_exit`, one pair of boxes per call, exactly as deep as the recursion goes.

## Turning on tracing for a selector

`AL.trace(selector)` prints every `Call`/`Exit`/`Redo`/`Fail` port as that selector runs, until `AL.notrace()` turns it back off. Since this notebook runs AL in its own process, the output shows up right below the cell. (If you're instead attached to a separate, persistent AL node, the trace prints to that node's own terminal, not the notebook.)

```elixir
AL.trace(:cell)

run do
  new(:cell, %{name: :traced}, c)
end

AL.notrace()
```

`Fail` shows up the same way when a call doesn't pan out — here `member` tries `:a` against `:z`, fails, then `:b` against `:z`, fails too.

```elixir
AL.trace(:list_member)

run do
  member([:a, :b], :z)
end

AL.notrace()
```

There's also a `vm_trace: true` option on `run` that interleaves the raw goals themselves into the same trace, alongside the `Call`/`Exit` events. It is a deeper knob than most debugging needs, but there if you want to see every goal AL actually executed, not just the method/clause ports.

Dispatch on an open receiver can have several candidate legs- a value/ephemeral leg and a durable leg, at minimum. Tracing shows which legs are being considered before a match is even found.

```elixir
run do
  defclass :trace_leg_class, super: :value, ivars: [] do
    defmethod(:trace_next, [
      %{class: :trace_leg_class, letter: :a},
      %{class: :trace_leg_class, letter: :b}
    ])
  end
end

AL.trace(:trace_next)

run do
  trace_next(x, %{class: :trace_leg_class, letter: :b})
end

AL.notrace()
```

## The derivation tree: a cleaner picture than raw trace text

`AL.Trace.derivation_tree/1` turns a run's trace into real nested nodes instead of indented text you have to read by eye. 
```elixir
{:atomic, {bindings, state}} =
  run do
    fibonacci(3, x)
  end

[root] = state.domino.trace |> Enum.reverse() |> AL.Trace.derivation_tree()

{root.kind, root.label, root.derived, length(root.children)}
```

When a method has more than one clause that could structurally match, redo tries the next one in place. The tree keeps only the attempt that actually won, not a trail of every clause that was tried.

```elixir
run do
  defclass :redo_demo, super: :object, ivars: [] do
  end

  defmethod(:redo_demo, :pick, [self, :first])
  defmethod(:redo_demo, :pick, [self, :second])
end
```

```elixir
{:atomic, {bindings, state}} =
  run do
    new(:redo_demo, %{}, obj)
    pick(obj, result)
    unify(result, :second)
  end

roots = state.domino.trace |> Enum.reverse() |> AL.Trace.derivation_tree()
pick_node = Enum.find(roots, &match?(%{label: {_, :pick, _}}, &1))

{pick_node.children, pick_node.derived}
```

The first clause (`:first`) is attempted and then upon fail, redone into the second clause. The derivation tree though, only shows the winner. It prunes the failed branches.

```elixir
run do
  defclass :probe_box, super: :object, ivars: [] do
    defmethod(:probe_reject, [self, v]) do
      unify(v, 1)
      v > 50
    end

    defmethod(:probe_leaf, [self, 100])

    defmethod(:probe_mid, [self, v]) do
      probe_leaf(self, w)
      vm_is(v, w + 1)
    end

    defmethod(:probe_top, [self, v]) do
      probe_mid(self, w)
      vm_is(v, w + 1)
    end

    defmethod(:probe_answer, [self, v]) do
      alternative([probe_reject(self, v)], [probe_top(self, v)])
    end
  end
end
```

```elixir
{:atomic, {bindings, state}} =
  run do
    new(:probe_box, %{}, obj)
    probe_answer(obj, r)
  end

roots = state.domino.trace |> Enum.reverse() |> AL.Trace.derivation_tree()
answer = Enum.find(roots, &match?(%{label: {_, :probe_answer, _}}, &1))

[top] = answer.children
[mid] = top.children
[leaf] = mid.children

{top.label, mid.label, leaf.label, Enum.any?(roots, &match?(%{label: {_, :probe_reject, _}}, &1))}
```

## Constraints show up in the tree too, nested under their own scope, with resolved values

Constraint goals (from [logic and constraints](./logic_and_constraints.livemd)) get their own node kind nested under whichever method scope posted them. Passing the run's final store into `derivation_tree/2` resolves that node to the value it settled on.

```elixir
run do
  defclass :triple_class, super: :object, ivars: [] do
    defmethod(:triple, [self, n, result]) do
      eq(result, n * 3)
    end
  end
end
```

```elixir
{:atomic, {bindings, state}} =
  run do
    new(:triple_class, %{}, obj)
    triple(obj, 4, r)
  end

roots =
  state.domino.trace
  |> Enum.reverse()
  |> AL.Trace.derivation_tree(state.active_choicepoint.store)

root = Enum.find(roots, &match?(%{label: {_, :triple, _}}, &1))
[constraint_node] = Enum.filter(root.children, &(&1.kind == :constraint))

{constraint_node.kind, constraint_node.derived}
```
